            <ArticleTitle>A fatal case of adult small bowel volvulus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>300-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.legalmed.2014.05.007</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1344-6223(14)00071-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 56-year-old man was hit by a car while riding a bicycle, immediately brought to the hospital, and treated. Four days after the accident, he reported abdominal pain and vomiting in the night, and he died the next day. A forensic autopsy was performed, and almost of the entire small bowel had gangrenous swelling. The mesentery was twisted 180° with clockwise rotation along the vascular axis, and pre-autopsy computed tomography scan showed a whirl sign.</AbstractText>
